lesson:
Interfaces share a *contract*. **Inheritance** shares *code*. When several classes have the same fields and most of the same behaviour, write it once in a parent class and let the others `extend` it.

```
abstract class Enemy {
    protected final String name;
    protected int health;

    protected Enemy(String name, int health) {
        this.name = name;
        this.health = health;
    }

    public void takeDamage(int amount) { health = Math.max(0, health - amount); }
    public boolean isAlive() { return health > 0; }
    public abstract int attackPower();      // each kind decides
}

class Slime extends Enemy {
    Slime() { super("Slime", 20); }
    @Override public int attackPower() { return 3; }
}
```

The pieces:
- `extends Enemy`: Slime gets every field and method of Enemy. A class can extend only **one** class.
- `super(...)` calls the parent's constructor. It must be the first line. If you forget it and the parent has no empty constructor, it won't compile.
- `abstract` class: you can't do `new Enemy(...)`; it only exists to be extended. An `abstract` method has no body, and every (non-abstract) child **must** write it.
- `@Override` marks a method that replaces the parent's version. Always write it: if you misspell the name, the compiler tells you instead of silently making a new method.
- `super.method()` calls the parent's version from inside an override, so you can **add to** it rather than copy it: `return super.describe() + " [ranged]";`.
- `protected`: visible to child classes (and the same package), but not to the whole world.

**Template method**: the parent writes the steps once (`fire()` checks ammo, uses ammo, returns damage) and calls abstract methods (`damage()`, `ammoPerShot()`) for the parts that differ. Children fill in the blanks and can't break the steps (make `fire()` `final`).

**When not to use inheritance.** Use it only for a real "is a" relationship: a Slime *is an* Enemy. If you're tempted to extend a class just to reuse one method, use **composition** instead: give your class a field of that type. A `Tractor` *has an* `Engine`; it isn't one. Deep chains (A extends B extends C extends D) get hard to change, because a change at the top ripples down. Many teams prefer small interfaces plus composition, and keep inheritance shallow: one abstract base and a few children.

Common mistakes: calling an overridable method from a constructor; forgetting `@Override`; making fields `public` instead of `protected`/`private`; copying the parent's code into an override instead of calling `super`.

example.code:
abstract class Animal {
    protected final String name;
    protected Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public abstract String sound();

    public String describe() { return name + " says " + sound(); }
}

class Cow extends Animal {
    Cow(String name) { super(name); }
    @Override public String sound() { return "moo"; }
}

class Goat extends Animal {
    Goat(String name) { super(name); }
    @Override public String sound() { return "meh"; }
    @Override public String describe() { return super.describe() + " (and eats everything)"; }
}

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal[] herd = { new Cow("Daisy"), new Goat("Kipchoge") };
        for (Animal a : herd) System.out.println(a.describe());
    }
}

